From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ilya Vysotsky <il.mohoff2016@yandex.ru>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 06:29:07 +0400
Subject: [PATCH] ANGLE on Windows 10 Mobile: the swap chain's buffers are made
 once, at the window's size

The keyboard and the system's bars change the panel's size all the time,
and every change was a ResizeBuffers: three buffers of 13-14 MB, each
wanted in one piece of address space, which an HP Elite x3 with 27 MB the
largest free piece refused. Build 181 made the refusal survivable; this
takes the request away.

The shell hands over the window's size as a capacity
(angle_uwp_set_panel_capacity, with a mode). A chain of a window drawn into
directly is made with buffers that size, the surface is their top-left
part, and a change of size that the buffers hold only changes how much of
them is shown: IDXGISwapChain2::SetSourceSize, given to the chain right
before the next present, which is a whole one. The back buffer is cleared
when the surface grows. mWidth and mHeight stay the surface's size;
mBufferWidth and mBufferHeight are the buffers'. Mode 2 adds a transform
that undoes a stretch of the shown part, for a Windows that turns out to
stretch it; mode 0, or no capacity, is the chain as it was.

What can be refused is survived: buffers at the capacity that cannot be
made are made at the surface's size; a ResizeBuffers that fails is tried
again, then at the surface's size, then put back; a source size that is
refused ends the use of one for that surface, and when the buffers are
larger than the surface the chain is made again at once at the surface's
size, so that no frame is drawn without a render target. Where a chain
cannot be made again at once (this path, or build 181's after a failed
resize) the frames drawn meanwhile no longer hand Direct3D a null view to
clear: Clear11 skips a window's surface that has no chain.

The panel's size and the capacity are each one atomic value: as two, a read
between the stores at rotation gave a size no window has, and buffers made
that size would now be kept.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
